Fixtures of the Grype wrapper (grype_test.go). Nothing here comes from a private source: the SBOM
describes public Alpine packages, and the other files are Grype's own output for it.

  sbom.json            the SBOM: Syft's JSON for two packages of an Alpine root, reduced as the
                       scanner reduces it for the hub (no relationships, no file lists)
  grype-output.json    what `grype sbom:sbom.json --by-cve -o json` printed for it, with jq's
                       indentation instead of Grype's single line
  db-status.json       what `grype db status -o json` printed for a valid database
  db-status-none.json  what it printed, with exit status 1, when the directory held no database

Versions
  Syft   v1.55.0, JSON schema 16.1.11 (the scanner's pin, Dockerfile.scanner)
  Grype  v0.121.0 (the matcher's pin, Dockerfile.matcher), database schema v6.1.10, built
         2026-10-30T06:23:28Z

Grype's answer follows its database, so a new run gives other matches. grype-output.json is what it
said on 2026-10-30. It has four matches, which is why the SBOM keeps two packages:

  brotli-libs 1.0.9-r1  CVE-2026-111497  Critical  unknown  no fixed version  known exploited
  musl 1.1.24-r8        CVE-2020-28928   Medium    fixed    1.1.24-r10
                        CVE-2025-26519   High      unknown
                        CVE-2026-40200   High      (no state)

Decision 16, known exploitation: Grype's own field is vulnerability.knownExploited, a list of the
CISA catalog's entries, and null when there are none. Only the first match has one. The matcher reads
that field, so it has no feed of its own (there is no kev.go).

How to make the files again

1. A root: the linux/amd64 image of Alpine 3.12.0, with brotli-libs added. The 3.12 repository's
   last brotli-libs is 1.0.9-r1, and the rest of the root is as old as the 3.12.0 image, so that the
   Alpine advisories have fixes to report. docker export writes the root into a volume, because the
   files of Alpine's root don't fit every host file system.

     docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t matcher-golden-root - <<'EOF'
     FROM alpine@sha256:a15790640a6690aa1730c38cf0a440e2aa44aaca9b0e8931a9f2b0d7cc90fd65
     RUN apk add --no-cache brotli-libs
     EOF
     docker volume create matcher-golden-root
     docker create --platform linux/amd64 --name matcher-golden-root matcher-golden-root
     docker export matcher-golden-root | docker run --rm -i -v matcher-golden-root:/r alpine:3.22 tar -x -C /r
     docker rm matcher-golden-root

2. Syft, the way the scanner runs it (cmd/scanner), on that root:

     docker run --rm -e SYFT_CHECK_FOR_APP_UPDATE=false -v matcher-golden-root:/root-apk:ro \
       anchore/syft:v1.55.0@sha256:4cc92b64ca74ffca4c94d4c8fcc01f217731375300be14d0b6eeb2e82eaa110a \
       scan dir:/root-apk --base-path /root-apk --override-default-catalogers image \
       -o syft-json --quiet > full.json

3. Reduce it as the scanner does, and keep what Grype needs for two packages: no relationships, no
   file lists, no maintainer (a person's address), and only the name and version of Syft's
   descriptor.

     jq 'del(.artifactRelationships, .files) | .descriptor |= {name, version}
         | .artifacts |= (map(select(.name == "brotli-libs" or .name == "musl"))
                          | map(del(.metadata.files, .metadata.maintainer)))' full.json > sbom.json

4. Grype, from the matcher's pin. The build stage of Dockerfile.matcher holds the Grype release and,
   with WITH_DB=true, the database (the binary is under /grype/<architecture>/). The environment is
   the one the matcher gives the CLI. This directory is the working directory of all four steps.

     docker build -f ../../../Dockerfile.matcher --target grype --build-arg WITH_DB=true \
       -t grype-golden ../../..
     docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/w:ro" \
       -e GRYPE_DB_AUTO_UPDATE=false -e GRYPE_CHECK_FOR_APP_UPDATE=false -e GRYPE_DB_VALIDATE_AGE=false \
       -e GRYPE_DB_CACHE_DIR=/opt/grype-db \
       grype-golden /grype/arm64/grype sbom:/w/sbom.json --by-cve -o json | jq . > grype-output.json

   (/grype/amd64/grype on an amd64 host. The status files are `grype db status -o json` in the
   same container; db-status-none.json comes from a GRYPE_DB_CACHE_DIR that holds no database.)

Steps 1 to 3 give the same sbom.json every time. Step 4 gives the same grype-output.json for the same
database, but for the timestamp in its descriptor, which is when Grype ran.
